+## Service-to-service communication
+
+Every channel between two Lakeside Mutual services is secured with mutual TLS (mTLS). Each service owns a key pair issued by a private certificate authority, the *Lakeside Mutual Internal CA*, and each service trusts only that CA. Both peers of a channel therefore prove their identity to each other before any application data is exchanged:
+
+| Channel | Transport | Server | Client |
+| ------- | --------- | ------ | ------ |
+| Customer data | HTTPS, port 8110 | [Customer Core](customer-core) | [Customer Self-Service Backend](customer-self-service-backend), [Customer Management Backend](customer-management-backend), [Policy Management Backend](policy-management-backend) |
+| Insurance quote events | OpenWire over TLS, port 61616 | [Policy Management Backend](policy-management-backend) (ActiveMQ broker) | [Customer Self-Service Backend](customer-self-service-backend), [Policy Management Backend](policy-management-backend) |
+| Policy events | STOMP over TLS, port 61613 | [Policy Management Backend](policy-management-backend) (ActiveMQ broker) | [Risk Management Server](risk-management-server) |
+| Customer data report | gRPC over TLS, port 50051 | [Risk Management Server](risk-management-server) | [Risk Management Client](risk-management-client) |
+
+The key material is generated by `./scripts/generate-certificates.sh` and is not checked into this repository; the script writes it to the place where each service expects it and the `.gitignore` file keeps it out of version control. Certificate authentication complements the application-level authentication that was already in place (API keys, JWT tokens and the broker's username/password) rather than replacing it.
+
+Two consequences are worth knowing about:
+
+- Because the Customer Core requires a client certificate, a browser cannot open its Swagger UI, its H2 console or its Thymeleaf pages without importing a client certificate first. To reach the Customer Core from the command line, pass a certificate to `curl`; the certificate generation script prints a ready-made example.
+- The channels between the browser-based frontends and their backends (ports 8080, 8090 and 8100) are *not* mutual TLS. A browser cannot present a service certificate, and these are not service-to-service channels.
+- [Spring Boot Admin](spring-boot-admin) is an optional development convenience that runs with all security disabled, and its monitoring channel is unchanged. Since it has no client certificate, it can no longer scrape the Customer Core's actuator endpoints and will show that service as offline.
+
